WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:10.629
Malcolm Grant: Why do we not move on to the Chief Executive’s report? Simon, over to you.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.679
Simon Stevens: Thanks, Malcolm. I will be
fairly brief, given that a number of the items

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:19.960
that we are going to substantively discuss
are things that I would otherwise refer to,

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:26.449
particularly the STP process and some of the
performance issues, at the start of the new

00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:33.150
financial year. However, firstly to say we
are obviously now in a position to take stock

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:39.760
of the 2015/2016 performance across the NHS
as a whole, and there is absolutely no doubt

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.770
that this was a very pressurised year. We
will talk in detail about that in the item

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:54.000
that Richard will be discussing. I do think
it is, however, worth reflecting that the

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.680
performance of the NHS in 2015/2016 still,
I think, on the headline measures, compares

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:06.060
very favourably with that of any other major
industrialised country’s healthcare system.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:14.360
The fact is that nine out of ten patients
being seen within A&amp;E in four hours is a remarkable

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:19.710
achievement. The fact is that nine out of
ten patients having their operations in under

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:29.090
18 weeks is something that we would have found
staggering even ten years ago. The fact is

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:36.250
that 1,000 or so people are waiting 52 weeks
compares with the 200,000 people who were

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:42.189
waiting a year for their care when I joined
the NHS in 1988. So we have come on an enormous

00:01:42.189 --> 00:01:47.210
distance. We should pay great thanks to the
front‑line staff across the NHS, who are

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:50.799
delivering these kinds of performances under
very difficult circumstances; none of which

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:55.270
is to deny that actually, particularly towards
the end of the year, as we will come on to

00:01:55.270 --> 00:02:02.850
discuss – February, March – we saw increasing
pressure and real stress in parts of the service.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:08.170
So the question, then, is what are we doing
about that in the year ahead? What does 2016/2017

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:13.670
look like? We go into this year in a much
stronger financial position as the National

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:18.760
Health Service as a whole. The front-loaded
spending review settlement means that, for

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:25.389
the first time in a number of years, the tariff
prices that are paid to hospitals will go

00:02:25.389 --> 00:02:32.949
up. The cost pressures in the system around
pensions and other aspects have been funded.

00:02:32.949 --> 00:02:40.690
The efficiency requirement has been set at
a more realistic headline level. However,

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:47.269
we have spent the last four to six weeks in
a complex situation, ensuring that the level

00:02:47.269 --> 00:02:54.169
of activity that is being planned and funded
through the Health Service is at realistic

00:02:54.169 --> 00:03:01.350
levels for both emergencies and for routine
operations. On the back of that, I think we

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:08.870
have made enormous progress in almost every
part of the country now, with agreements finalised

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:15.239
for both local CCG-commissioned activity and
for specialised activity in the vast, vast

00:03:15.239 --> 00:03:20.889
majority of cases. I think we are in a better
position, as we sit here in May this year,

00:03:20.889 --> 00:03:27.729
than we were in May last. I think we feel
confident that the deficits that have been

00:03:27.729 --> 00:03:32.459
reported will come down very, very substantially
during the course of this year compared to

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:38.530
last and that, together with NHS Improvement,
the agreements that they are entering into

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:47.019
with individual trusts should secure improved
A&amp;E performance by next March compared to

00:03:47.019 --> 00:03:50.660
the position that we have just seen this past
March.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:04:01.900
However, the fact is that the individual organisational
financial plans, despite having moved in the

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.229
right direction over the last month or six
weeks, there is still work that NHS Improvement

00:04:06.229 --> 00:04:11.560
have under way with the trust sector to ensure
that there is a balanced plan going into the

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:20.370
year, and Jim Mackey will be updating their
board at their public meeting on that today.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:25.100
It means that, over the course of the next
three or four weeks, NHS Improvement will

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:29.190
be having more detailed conversations with
trusts about what their control totals need

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:35.720
to be, so that we go into the year with a
balanced position, and such contingency as

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:41.030
there is can be used genuinely for pressures
that are raised unexpectedly, as against for

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:46.510
an imbalanced position at the start of the
year. That is not going to be a completely

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:54.400
straightforward exercise, to ensure that people
are able to have control total plans that

00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:01.780
they can easily deliver. Nobody underestimates
that, but the fact is we have seen very substantial

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:05.520
cost growth in a number of areas over the
course of the last 12 and 24 months, and we

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.780
are going to have to tackle some of that at
source.

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:18.720
More broadly, the process that Jo Lenaghan
will talk us through around the local conversations

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.760
– the 44 local conversations on the sustainability
and transformation plans – I think are hugely

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:32.010
encouraging, in that the NHS partners in local
government and other parts of the care system

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:38.330
are now having very frank and constructive
conversations about the big changes that will

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:46.750
be necessary in order to put us on the track
to deliver against the three aims of the Forward

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:52.690
View: improved health and wellbeing; improved
care quality and redesign; and financial sustainability.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.950
We will hear more about that from Jo, but
that is going to be an absolutely essential

00:05:55.950 --> 00:06:03.420
process to get right between now and the summer
break, and then the subsequent process into

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.070
the autumn.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:15.430
Couple of other items. I think we had a strong
launch of the GP Forward View, with a good,

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:23.040
concrete set of proposals, both to help support
GPs now as well as to make some of the wider

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:28.200
and more profound changes in primary care
that everybody can see are required. Of course,

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:35.770
this is not the only set of things that needs
doing. It is not the final answer to all of

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:42.080
the pressures in general practice. However,
if implemented effectively, I think the consensus

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.770
view, including from the Royal College of
GPs – and, indeed, the leadership of the

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:49.700
General Practitioners Committee – is that
this has the potential to make a huge difference

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:57.520
for patients and for GPs. So, Arvind Madan,
Ros Roughton and her partners now, sleeves

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:01.080
rolled up, get going on implementing that.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:08.139
This morning, the National Audit Office have
published an important report on the impact

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:13.360
that the latest charges are having on the
NHS, pointing once again to the pressures

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:18.970
that have been building in social care. At
the time of the NHS Five Year Forward View,

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:25.560
obviously we pointed out that the ability
of the NHS to deliver was highly interrelated

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:32.490
to the continuing availability of a well-resourced
and well-functioning adult social care system.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:40.260
There are ways in which that interface can
be improved, and the STP process is part of

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:44.460
the conversation for doing that, but there
are some broader unresolved issues in respect

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:49.060
of social care that, clearly, the country
will have to come back to.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:57.580
Finally, Malcolm, just to mention a couple
of other points that have arisen in the last

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:02.310
few days but which may not have received the
attention they deserve. We spend a lot of

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:09.139
our time talking about pressures in A&amp;Es and
other elements of the work of the Health Service,

00:08:09.139 --> 00:08:14.650
but of course the reality is that what the
NHS does is far, far wider than that. So two

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:21.590
things just to bring to the Board’s attention.
Firstly, within the last week or so, we have

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:28.180
just published the latest cancer survival
data, and you will see a further improvement

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:34.449
in cancer survival rates, both one-year and
five-year – at an all-time high now. Your

00:08:34.449 --> 00:08:42.440
survival chances are now 70% within a year
of having a cancer diagnosis and treatment,

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:47.380
compared with 60% slightly over ten years
ago.

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:54.760
Also, this morning saw publication of important
data around improved anti-microbial prescribing

00:08:54.760 --> 00:09:00.650
by GPs. Obviously, at a time of a lot of pressure
in general practice, it is hugely encouraging

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:08.860
that we saw a 7% reduction in prescribing
for antibiotics that were not required, and

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:14.050
a 16% reduction in broad-spectrum antibiotics,
given that antibiotic resistance is one of

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:18.670
the huge health threats facing all countries’
healthcare systems, killing 50,000 people

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:25.339
a year in Europe and the United States. This
shows that the NHS, actually, is a world leader

00:09:25.339 --> 00:09:31.830
on tackling antimicrobial resistance. So,
just two very specific examples, but illustrative

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:35.790
of a much broader story about the things that
are happening across the National Health Service,

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:39.640
that often do not get the focus and the attention
that they actually deserve.

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:44.490
Malcolm Grant: Simon, thank you very much
for that report. May I invite any questions

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:48.890
from members of the Board? No? If not, thank
you, Simon.

